987
01:34:57.160 --> 01:35:01.946
A schedule can make sequence, timing and
change visible. It gives students

988
01:35:01.947 --> 01:35:06.929
information they can return to, rather
than relying entirely on listening and

989
01:35:06.930 --> 01:35:11.584
remembering. Schedules can cover different
spans: a first-then sequence,

990
01:35:11.585 --> 01:35:16.502
one activity, part of the day or the whole
day. Choose a format the intended

991
01:35:16.503 --> 01:35:21.682
student can access and understand. The
question is whether it shows their actual

992
01:35:21.683 --> 01:35:26.534
day, including choices, pauses and
changes, or only the tasks adults expect

993
01:35:26.535 --> 01:35:31.255
them to complete. Make flexibility visible
too. If something changes, how

994
01:35:31.256 --> 01:35:36.107
does the schedule show that? Does it still
provide useful information after

995
01:35:36.108 --> 01:35:40.894
the original plan has changed? Can the
student see what comes next without

996
01:35:40.895 --> 01:35:45.549
needing another spoken explanation? A
schedule is useful when it reduces

997
01:35:45.550 --> 01:35:50.663
uncertainty and supports participation for
the person using it. Its presence on

998
01:35:50.664 --> 01:35:55.712
the wall or desk isn’t enough. Watch it in
use, invite feedback, and adjust it

999
01:35:55.713 --> 01:35:58.925
when it no longer answers the student’s
