1290
02:00:09.825 --> 02:00:11.590
twenty-three. Let's watch.

1291
02:02:04.040 --> 02:02:08.860
What impact did those decisions have on
Adon? Take a moment to consider what you

1292
02:02:08.861 --> 02:02:13.742
heard, and what might have been different
if Adon's perspective had been included

1293
02:02:13.743 --> 02:02:18.503
in the decisions. Now bring the question
back to the students you have in mind.

1294
02:02:18.504 --> 02:02:23.202
How are their preferences asked for and
heard? Is there a way for a student to

1295
02:02:23.203 --> 02:02:28.145
tell you that something isn't working, as
well as to choose between options adults

1296
02:02:28.146 --> 02:02:32.600
have already offered? Pause here if you'd
like time to reflect or discuss.

1297
02:02:40.600 --> 02:02:44.975
Students need a say in decisions about
them, in ways that work with their

1298
02:02:44.976 --> 02:02:49.716
communication. Even a young child can
express a preference. We can offer visual

1299
02:02:49.717 --> 02:02:54.700
choices, attend to a response, and honour
what it tells us. Questions such as, what

1300
02:02:54.701 --> 02:02:59.381
helps, what's hard, and what would you
like teachers to know, can open up that

1301
02:02:59.382 --> 02:03:04.243
understanding. We may need to change the
format, the setting or the time we allow

1302
02:03:04.244 --> 02:03:08.620
for an answer. Where a student uses words,
record their words accurately.

1303
02:03:08.621 --> 02:03:13.117
Where they communicate in other ways, make
clear what they communicated and

1304
02:03:13.118 --> 02:03:17.494
how. Keep adult interpretation visible as
interpretation. Including their

1305
02:03:17.495 --> 02:03:21.870
perspective in the record, and acting on
it, helps build a foundation for

1306
02:03:21.871 --> 02:03:22.720
self-advocacy.

1307
02:03:24.240 --> 02:03:29.365
Turn to the Partnership activity grid on
page twenty-four. This gives us a way to

1308
02:03:29.366 --> 02:03:34.556
look at the people in a partnership with a
little more care. Work through the four

1309
02:03:34.557 --> 02:03:39.297
partners: the student, parents and carers,
teachers, and paraprofessionals.

1310
02:03:39.298 --> 02:03:44.231
For each, note what they bring and the
challenges they might face. Think about

1311
02:03:44.232 --> 02:03:48.845
knowledge, relationships, time,
communication and the demands each person

1312
02:03:48.846 --> 02:03:53.394
may be managing. Avoid assuming that every
person in a role has the same

1313
02:03:53.395 --> 02:03:58.008
experience. Then compare your four
sections. What do they have in common?

1314
02:03:58.009 --> 02:04:02.878
Where do you see different knowledge or
different needs? What could help them

1315
02:04:02.879 --> 02:04:07.684
work together? Give yourselves eight
minutes for the full activity. Everyone

1316
02:04:07.685 --> 02:04:12.746
can contribute in a way that suits them,
and you can write or reflect quietly as

1317
02:04:12.747 --> 02:04:17.680
well as talk. Keep the student at the
centre of the grid. Pause the video now,

1318
02:04:17.681 --> 02:04:19.410
and return for the debrief.

1319
02:04:27.440 --> 02:04:32.076
Let's draw that together. Look across your
grid for similarities and

1320
02:04:32.077 --> 02:04:37.198
differences. Which strengths might
complement one another? Which challenges

1321
02:04:37.199 --> 02:04:42.250
could the partnership itself help address?
A useful next question is, what

1322
02:04:42.251 --> 02:04:47.095
would make this partnership more
successful? Perhaps you need a clearer

1323
02:04:47.096 --> 02:04:52.562
shared purpose, a different communication
arrangement, or a way for one person's

1324
02:04:52.563 --> 02:04:57.961
knowledge to be heard earlier. Use the
circumstances you've identified to guide

1325
02:04:57.962 --> 02:05:03.221
that choice. Return to our collaboration
principles: shared goals and values,

1326
02:05:03.222 --> 02:05:07.996
trust and respect, consultation, curiosity
and student voice. Assuming

1327
02:05:07.997 --> 02:05:12.564
good intent about outcomes for the student
can help us begin, while

1328
02:05:12.565 --> 02:05:16.993
listening tells us what each person
actually needs and hopes for.

1329
02:05:16.994 --> 02:05:22.461
Understanding each partner's strengths and
challenges gives trust and confidence

1330
02:05:22.462 --> 02:05:24.745
something practical to grow from.

1331
02:05:26.280 --> 02:05:31.332
Let's draw on relationships that already
work. Still on page twenty-four, find

1332
02:05:31.333 --> 02:05:36.451
What works? In your school group, think of
a family with whom you have, or have

1333
02:05:36.452 --> 02:05:41.439
had, a good relationship. How did you
establish it? What does it look like in

1334
02:05:41.440 --> 02:05:46.164
practice? Why does it work? Try to
identify the actual moves that helped:

1335
02:05:46.165 --> 02:05:51.283
what someone did before a meeting, how
information was shared, or how a concern

1336
02:05:51.284 --> 02:05:56.598
was handled. Keep the example anonymous,
and stay with what you can learn from the

1337
02:05:56.599 --> 02:06:01.258
relationship. If you're working alone,
make a few notes against the same

1338
02:06:01.259 --> 02:06:05.655
questions. The activity allows six
minutes. Pause the video for that

1339
02:06:05.656 --> 02:06:10.183
reflection and conversation. When you
return, we'll connect it to some

1340
02:06:10.184 --> 02:06:13.136
practices that can help everyone at the
table.

1341
02:06:21.160 --> 02:06:26.117
Let's come back. Choose one idea from your
reflection that you could carry into

1342
02:06:26.118 --> 02:06:30.885
another relationship. The list here gives
us some practical starting points:

1343
02:06:30.886 --> 02:06:35.399
prepare, share information, ask questions,
seek clarification and assume

1344
02:06:35.400 --> 02:06:40.612
the best. On the school side, preparation
includes sending the agenda beforehand so

1345
02:06:40.613 --> 02:06:45.379
the family knows what to expect, and
inviting their input. Welcome a support

1346
02:06:45.380 --> 02:06:50.083
person. That can make it easier for
someone to contribute and keep track of

1347
02:06:50.084 --> 02:06:54.724
what is being discussed. Open with a
strength. Share notes or minutes, and

1348
02:06:54.725 --> 02:06:59.746
follow up in writing so people can check
what was agreed. An opening such as, as

1349
02:06:59.747 --> 02:07:04.196
we agreed, is only useful when the record
actually reflects that shared

1350
02:07:04.197 --> 02:07:09.155
understanding. When a meeting has gone
badly, acknowledging that experience and

1351
02:07:09.156 --> 02:07:14.049
offering a reset can help. Ask what would
make the next meeting fairer or more

1352
02:07:14.050 --> 02:07:18.499
accessible. Families continue to support
learning across many settings.

1353
02:07:18.500 --> 02:07:23.013
Recognising their knowledge and
contribution means making room for it to

1354
02:07:23.014 --> 02:07:24.920
shape the work we do together.

1355
02:07:26.440 --> 02:07:31.721
Meeting families where they are begins
with listening before solving. Ask about

1356
02:07:31.722 --> 02:07:37.206
communication preferences and priorities.
Make room for questions and uncertainty,

1357
02:07:37.207 --> 02:07:41.878
including questions we haven't
anticipated. Explain roles, options and

1358
02:07:41.879 --> 02:07:47.159
next steps in plain language. Check what
is workable for the family now. A plan

1359
02:07:47.160 --> 02:07:51.967
that looks simple within a school
timetable may interact with demands we

1360
02:07:51.968 --> 02:07:57.452
don't know about. Avoid judging the pace
of a response, the choices someone makes,

1361
02:07:57.453 --> 02:08:02.666
or the time they need to consider an
option. This brings our environment-first

1362
02:08:02.667 --> 02:08:07.880
thinking into the meeting itself. Where
might the school's language, timing or

1363
02:08:07.881 --> 02:08:13.229
process create a barrier? What could we
change? A different format, questions in

1364
02:08:13.230 --> 02:08:18.240
advance, a quieter place, or clearer
follow-up may change the experience of

1365
02:08:18.241 --> 02:08:23.657
consultation. Keep asking what you need to
understand. Listening and clarity give

1366
02:08:23.658 --> 02:08:27.043
us a stronger basis for solving something
together.

1367
02:08:28.560 --> 02:08:33.303
On page twenty-five you'll find the
Consultation checklist. You can use it

1368
02:08:33.304 --> 02:08:37.982
before a meeting, and return to it
afterwards. Start with a clear, shared

1369
02:08:37.983 --> 02:08:43.116
purpose. What are we trying to understand
or decide together? Use open questions

1370
02:08:43.117 --> 02:08:48.055
that allow an answer you haven't already
chosen. Provide more than one way to

1371
02:08:48.056 --> 02:08:53.384
contribute, and respect the knowledge the
student and family bring. Record what you

1372
02:08:53.385 --> 02:08:57.868
hear accurately. It can help to check your
understanding aloud: have I

1373
02:08:57.869 --> 02:09:02.937
understood that correctly? Then agree an
action, who is responsible and how you

1374
02:09:02.938 --> 02:09:07.291
will follow up. If something remains
undecided, make that clear too.

1375
02:09:07.292 --> 02:09:12.035
Afterwards, ask yourself whether the
consultation changed or extended your

1376
02:09:12.036 --> 02:09:17.104
understanding. Could everyone contribute
in a workable way? Do people know what

1377
02:09:17.105 --> 02:09:22.303
happens next? Ask, listen and follow up. A
checklist or planning tool can help us

1378
02:09:22.304 --> 02:09:27.437
remember those steps, while the quality of
the consultation still depends on our

1379
02:09:27.438 --> 02:09:29.581
attention to the people involved.

1380
02:09:31.120 --> 02:09:36.375
Let's look first at consulting students.
Begin by presuming competence, and make

1381
02:09:36.376 --> 02:09:41.165
communication accessible. That might
involve augmentative and alternative

1382
02:09:41.166 --> 02:09:46.355
communication, visuals, writing, drawing,
video or symbols. Offer questions and

1383
02:09:46.356 --> 02:09:51.012
meeting information beforehand, simple
choices where useful, and enough

1384
02:09:51.013 --> 02:09:55.669
processing time. Notice the sensory
environment. Build on the student's

1385
02:09:55.670 --> 02:10:00.925
strengths and interests. Behaviour may
communicate preferences or difficulty, so

1386
02:10:00.926 --> 02:10:05.782
observe carefully and check your
interpretation wherever possible. Trusted

1387
02:10:05.783 --> 02:10:10.972
supporters can assist communication while
keeping the student's own perspective

1388
02:10:10.973 --> 02:10:16.095
central. What a student tells us needs to
influence what we do. With families,

1389
02:10:16.096 --> 02:10:21.151
begin with the student's strengths,
interests and what works. Listen to their

1390
02:10:21.152 --> 02:10:25.808
knowledge without assuming you already
know their experience. Use plain

1391
02:10:25.809 --> 02:10:30.333
language, explain school terms and respect
differences in culture and

1392
02:10:30.334 --> 02:10:34.790
perspective. Some parents may be
neurodivergent themselves, so check

1393
02:10:34.791 --> 02:10:39.714
communication preferences rather than
making assumptions. Use real examples

1394
02:10:39.715 --> 02:10:44.704
from school to discuss needs, goals,
participation and possible adjustments.

1395
02:10:44.705 --> 02:10:49.827
Check shared understanding, agree next
steps and keep the conversation open as

1396
02:10:49.828 --> 02:10:54.617
circumstances change. This extends the
consultation work we introduced in

1397
02:10:54.618 --> 02:10:59.740
Session Four. Choose one practice from
each column that you could try with the

1398
02:10:59.741 --> 02:11:03.998
student and family you have in mind. Pause
to note those choices.

1399
02:11:12.000 --> 02:11:16.461
As you look at your two choices, check
what they would make easier for the

1400
02:11:16.462 --> 02:11:20.679
person involved. Can the student
communicate more comfortably? Can the

1401
02:11:20.680 --> 02:11:25.446
family prepare, ask a question or add
knowledge that might otherwise be missed?

1402
02:11:25.447 --> 02:11:30.336
Keep those choices with your conversation
notes. Consultation becomes more useful

1403
02:11:30.337 --> 02:11:34.615
when we adapt it to the people involved,
and then act on what we learn.

1404
02:11:36.120 --> 02:11:40.979
Partnership becomes particularly important
when a student's capacity runs

1405
02:11:40.980 --> 02:11:45.703
out. The terms on this slide are meltdown,
shutdown, and what the slide

1406
02:11:45.704 --> 02:11:51.103
calls School Can't. That is, the student
cannot attend school. We are considering

1407
02:11:51.104 --> 02:11:56.165
these through capacity, accumulated
demands and the parts of experience that

1408
02:11:56.166 --> 02:12:01.295
may be difficult for others to see. These
experiences need a response without

1409
02:12:01.296 --> 02:12:05.615
blame. Describing them as wilful
misbehaviour, or as a failure of

1410
02:12:05.616 --> 02:12:10.137
parenting, can close down the
understanding we need. Home and school

1411
02:12:10.138 --> 02:12:14.929
may see different parts of the student's
day. A difference between those

1412
02:12:14.930 --> 02:12:20.262
observations is a reason to listen and
learn more. Bring back the questions from

1413
02:12:20.263 --> 02:12:25.459
Sessions Two and Three. What has the day
been asking of this student? What may

1414
02:12:25.460 --> 02:12:30.656
have accumulated? What information does
the student have, and what might their

1415
02:12:30.657 --> 02:12:35.381
family know about recovery? Keep the focus
on safety, what preceded the

1416
02:12:35.382 --> 02:12:40.645
situation, what supports recovery, and
what can change in the environment, task

1417
02:12:40.646 --> 02:12:45.505
or response. Use the workshop's safety
guidance and the support processes

1418
02:12:45.506 --> 02:12:50.500
relevant to your setting. The partnership
task here is to share information

1419
02:12:50.501 --> 02:12:55.495
respectfully, listen carefully and
identify changes that may reduce demands

1420
02:12:55.496 --> 02:12:57.992
and improve the student's experience.

1421
02:12:59.520 --> 02:13:04.749
Here are two Positive Partnerships tools
that can help make those conversations

1422
02:13:04.750 --> 02:13:09.777
